{
  "moduleId": "m00190",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00190:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Other Aromatic Substitutions"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "279bf6af-5206-4255-a44b-464ab1e074a9"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00190:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Aromatic Halogenation"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "Toluene reacts with a fluorinating agent to form ortho-fluorotoluene and para-fluorotoluene in 3: 1 ratio and 82 percent combined yield."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OSX_OrgChem_16_02_Toulene_img.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "The structures of Sitagliptin (Januvia) and Fluoxetine (Prozac)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "Benzene reacts with molecular chlorine in the presence of iron trichloride catalyst to form chlorobenzene in 86 percent yield and hydrochloric acid as a byproduct. The structure of Loratadine is depicted."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "Iodine reacts with hydrogen peroxide or copper (2) chloride to generate I plus. Benzene reacts with I plus to form a carbocation, which on deprotonation by base forms iodobenzene in 65 percent yield."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "Tyrosine reacts with I plus in the presence of thyroid peroxidase to form 3,5-diiodotyrosine, which further forms thyroxine (a thyroid hormone)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00190:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Aromatic Nitration"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00007",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00007"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00190:caption:fig-00006-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "Nitric and sulfuric acids react to form water and nitronium ion. The latter reacts with benzene to form nitrobenzene and water. Ball-and-stick model in electrostatic potential map of nitronium ion is depicted."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00008",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00008"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "Nitrobenzene reacts with iron, and acid in first step and base in second step to form aniline in 95 percent yield."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00003",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00190:title:sect-00003-title",
        "text": "Aromatic Sulfonation"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00009",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00009"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00190:caption:fig-00008-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "A three-step reversible reaction shows benzene reacting with intermediate formed from sulfur trioxide and sulfuric acid to form benzenesulfonic acid. Ball-and-stick model in electrostatic potential map of intermediate is depicted."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00010",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00010"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "Sulfanilamide has benzene ring. C 1 is bonded to sulfur, which is double bonded to two oxygens and single bonded to amino. C 4 is bonded to an amino group."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00004",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00190:title:sect-00004-title",
        "text": "Aromatic Hydroxylation"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00011",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00011"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "Para-hydroxyphenylacetate reacts with molecular oxygen in the presence of para-hydroxyphenylacetate-3-hydroxylase to yield 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetate. In the product, an extra hydroxyl group gets added at C 3."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00012",
          "segmentId": "m00190:para:para-00012"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00011",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00190:caption:fig-00011-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00190:alt:fig-00011-alt",
              "text": "A three-step reaction shows F A D H 2 reacting with molecular oxygen to generate an intermediate which reacts with para-hydroxyphenylacetate to form 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetate."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_16_02_011.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
